=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk ticket's premise is incorrect — router16 is not down, and Campus 1 is not cut off. Direct inspection of the live lab shows the network is fully operational on all relevant subsystems.

**Router16 status:** All key interfaces are up. eth0 and eth1 are bonded into bond0 (10.2.2.1/24, connecting to mswitch0) and eth2 (172.30.1.1/24, connecting to router7) is administratively and operationally UP. No interface is unexpectedly down.

**OSPF adjacencies:** Router16 holds two Full OSPF adjacencies — one to mswitch0 (neighbor ID 172.16.30.254, via bond0, Full/Backup) and one to router7 (neighbor ID 192.168.10.1, via eth2, Full/DR). Router7's neighbor table confirms it sees router16 (172.30.1.1) as Full/Backup. mswitch0 confirms Full/DR with router16 as well. No neighbor is stuck in Init/2-Way/Exstart.

**Campus 1 route advertisement:** mswitch0 (the Campus 1 multilayer switch) has VLAN SVI interfaces vlan10 (172.16.10.254/24), vlan20 (172.16.20.254/24), and vlan30 (172.16.30.254/24) all UP, and its OSPF config correctly advertises all three stub networks. The OSPF link-state database on router16 contains mswitch0's router-LSA with all three 172.16.x.0/24 prefixes. Router7's RIB shows OSPF routes to all three Campus 1 subnets via 172.30.1.1 (router16).

**Data-plane confirmation:** router10 successfully pings 172.16.10.254, 172.16.20.254, and 172.16.30.254 with 0% packet loss. Campus 1 end-host pc5 (172.16.10.5) successfully pings 192.168.144.1 on the far side of the network with 0% loss.

**Root cause:** There is no fault. The operations report is a false alarm; router16 and the entire Campus 1 path are healthy and forwarding normally. No corrective action is required.
=== END ===